<BaseLayout title="Editorial Policy | Toolwise AI" description="How Toolwise AI approaches accuracy, usefulness, AI-assisted writing and updates."><article class="prose"><span class="eyebrow">Editorial standards</span><h1>Clarity is a practice.</h1><h2>Reader-first purpose</h2><p>Every article should answer a specific question, explain useful trade-offs and give readers a practical next step. We avoid publishing pages whose only purpose is to repeat keywords or create volume.</p><h2>AI-assisted work</h2><p>AI may assist with outlining, drafting, editing and organizing research. The publisher remains responsible for accuracy, originality, source quality and the final page. AI-generated text is not treated as proof.</p><h2>Product research and testing</h2><p>We distinguish vendor documentation, independent sources, user feedback and hands-on tests. We do not invent testing, benchmarks, credentials, customer outcomes or quotations. Change-sensitive claims should link to appropriate sources and be checked before publication.</p><h2>Corrections and updates</h2><p>When a material error is identified, we aim to correct it. Pricing, features and policies may change; check the current provider documentation before making a decision. Update dates should reflect substantive updates, not cosmetic edits.</p><h2>Commercial independence</h2><p>Affiliate relationships, if present, should be disclosed clearly. Commercial relationships do not guarantee a positive recommendation. We aim to explain who a tool may suit and where it may fall short.</p></article></BaseLayout>
